 "result_text": "Fixed: the columns now size correctly. `find_widths` in `src/pyRestTable/rest_table.py` measured each cell with `str(_ or '')`, so a falsy value like `0` or `0.0` counted as an empty string. The rows are drawn with `str(r)`, so those cells printed wider than the column. I changed the measurement to `str(_)` so both use the same text.\n\nI ran a two-column version of your example (`h` and `mu`, with `0`, `0.0` and `1` in the cells). Both columns come out three characters wide, and the rows line up with the borders. I didn't run your full nine-column example or the test suite.\n\nOne side effect: a `None` cell is now measured as `None` (4 characters), matching how it's drawn, rather than as empty.",
